\title{Continuity Depends on the Topology}
\taxon{Example}
\p{
    Put the Euclidean topology #{\tau_e} and the cofinite topology #{\tau_c}
    on the same underlying set #{\mathbb{R}}. The identity function
    ##{
        \operatorname{id}:(\mathbb{R},\tau_e)
        \longrightarrow(\mathbb{R},\tau_c)
    }
    is continuous. Indeed, the complement of every cofinite open set is
    finite. Singletons are Euclidean closed, and finite unions of closed sets
    remain closed, so every cofinite open set is Euclidean open.
}
\p{
    The identity in the reverse direction is not continuous. The interval
    #{(0,1)} is Euclidean open, but its preimage under the identity is not
    cofinite open because #{\mathbb{R}\setminus(0,1)} is infinite. Thus the
    same set-theoretic function can be continuous in one direction and
    discontinuous in the other.
}
